json_serialize(obj, flags = 0, /)

Serialize a Pyton object into a JSON string. By default, unsupported types are
serialized as `null` and dict keys which aren't strings are skipped.

Parameters
----------
obj
	A Python object.
flags : int, optional
	A set of integer flags which can be used to select an ouput format and to
        modify the handling of unsupported types and non-string dictionary keys.

	The following output formats are supported:

	UJ_FMT_FAST (0)
        	Serialize dict key-value pairs in the order they're returned by
                `PyDict_Next` and don't include any redundant whitespace in the
                output.

	UJ_FMT_DET (1)
        	Serialize dict key-value pairs in deterministic order. Dict keys
                will be sorted based on Unicode codepoint comparisons prior to
                serialization.

	UJ_FMT_PRETTY (2)
        	Like UJ_FMT_DET but include redundant whiteapce in the generated
                string to make it easier to read for humans. To avoid creating
                extremely huge strings mostly composed of space characters for
                large and complex input objects, indentation is done with sequences
                of TAB (ASCII 09) characters.

	Flags which can be ored into the value to modify the behaviour of the
        algorithm:

	UNK_TYPE_ERR (4)
        	Instead of serializing unsupported types as `null`, raise a
                ValueError upon encountering one.

	NONSTR_KEY_ERR (8)
        	Raise a ValueError when encountering a dict key which isn't
                a string.

	STRINGIFY_NONSTR_KEYS (16)
        	Serialize dict key - value pairs whose keys aren't strings
                by stringifying them via `PyObject_Str`.

	The last two are mutually exclusive but the code doesn't check for
        this. If both are specified, the latter will take precedence but
        this is an implementation detail not to be relied upon.

Returns
-------
string
	A JSON string corresponding to the object passed as first argument.

Raises
------
MemoryError
	A memory allocation failed while serializing.
ValueError
	An unsupported type or non-string dict key were encountered
        and raising an error for this situation had been requested
        by passing the corresponding flag value as part of the
        second argument.
